<style>
#form-ID-container {
	container-type: inline-size;
}
#form-ID {
	grid-template-areas:
		'enabled'
		'toolPrefix'
		'section-header-1'
		'publicAccess'
		'publicIpPerMinute'
		'allowedOrigins'
		'section-header-2'
		'subscriptionsEnabled'
		'subscriptionStreamSeconds'
		'section-header-3'
		'listeningStream'
		'listeningStreamSeconds'
		'listeningStreamRetryMs'
		'section-header-4'
		'listeningStreamMaxConcurrent';
	grid-template-columns: 1fr;
}
@container (min-width: 500px) {
	#form-ID {
		grid-template-areas:
			'enabled .'
			'toolPrefix .'
			'section-header-1  .'
			'publicAccess .'
			'publicIpPerMinute .'
			'allowedOrigins allowedOrigins'
			'section-header-2  .'
			'subscriptionsEnabled .'
			'subscriptionStreamSeconds .'
			'section-header-3  .'
			'listeningStream .'
			'listeningStreamSeconds listeningStreamRetryMs'
			'section-header-4  .'
			'listeningStreamMaxConcurrent .';
		grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
	}
}
</style><div id="form-ID-container"><form id="form-ID" class="totalform help-on-hover help-box formgrid" data-method="POST" data-route="/admin/settings/mcp"><h3 class="form-grid-section-header" style="grid-area: section-header-1;">Public Access</h3><h3 class="form-grid-section-header" style="grid-area: section-header-2;">Resource Subscriptions</h3><h3 class="form-grid-section-header" style="grid-area: section-header-3;">SSE Probe Responses</h3><h3 class="form-grid-section-header" style="grid-area: section-header-4;">Worker Budget</h3><div class="form-field toggle-field " data-type="toggle" style="--grid-area: enabled;"><label for="field-UID">Enable MCP Server</label><div class="form-group"><div class="switch"><input id="field-UID" name="enabled" type="checkbox" aria-describedby="help-UID" checked/><label for="field-UID" aria-hidden="true">Enable MCP Server</label></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">Master switch for the MCP endpoint. When off, <code>/mcp</code> returns 404 and discovery reports <code>disabled: true</code>. Defaults on for Pro+ editions.</p></div><div class="form-field toggle-field " data-type="toggle" style="--grid-area: publicAccess;"><label for="field-UID">Allow Public Access</label><div class="form-group"><div class="switch"><input id="field-UID" name="publicAccess" type="checkbox" aria-describedby="help-UID" checked/><label for="field-UID" aria-hidden="true">Allow Public Access</label></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">Allow anonymous (no API key) callers to discover and use tools whose collection has <code>mcp.access: 'public'</code> set on its schema. Turn this off to require an API key for every MCP request, regardless of collection settings.</p></div><div class="form-field list-field " data-type="list" style="--grid-area: allowedOrigins;" data-settings="{&quot;duplicateItemsAllowed&quot;:false,&quot;addChoices&quot;:true}"><label for="field-UID">CORS Allowed Origins</label><div class="form-group"><select id="field-UID" name="allowedOrigins" multiple size="1" aria-describedby="help-UID"><option value="https://chatgpt.com">ChatGPT</option><option value="https://claude.ai">Claude.ai</option><option value="https://copilot.microsoft.com">Microsoft Copilot</option><option value="https://gemini.google.com">Google Gemini</option><option value="https://chat.mistral.ai">Mistral Le Chat</option><option value="https://www.perplexity.ai">Perplexity</option><option value="*">* (any origin — use cautiously)</option></select><div class="form-group-icon"></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">Origins permitted to call <code>/mcp</code> from <strong>inside a browser tab</strong>. Pick a known browser-based AI client from the dropdown, or type a custom origin (e.g. <code>https://example.com</code>) for in-house playgrounds. <br><br><strong>Native MCP clients (Claude Desktop, Cursor, Claude Code, MCP Inspector) connect regardless of this setting</strong> — CORS is a browser-only mechanism and never reaches non-browser clients. Server-side AI integrations are also unaffected. <br><br>Leave empty (default) to allow <strong>any</strong> origin — equivalent to <code>*</code> — so browser-based AI clients work out of the box. To <strong>restrict</strong> browser access, list specific origins instead. Note: an open policy lets any website's JavaScript read your <em>public</em> MCP surface from a visitor's browser (it can never reach auth-gated collections); if you have public-but-sensitive collections, list explicit origins.</p></div><div class="form-field number-field  field-hidden" data-type="number" style="--grid-area: publicIpPerMinute; display: none;" data-settings="{&quot;visibility&quot;:{&quot;watch&quot;:&quot;publicAccess&quot;,&quot;value&quot;:true,&quot;operator&quot;:&quot;==&quot;}}"><label for="field-UID">Public Rate Limit (per minute)</label><div class="form-group"><input type="number" id="field-UID" name="publicIpPerMinute" inputmode="numeric" placeholder="60" aria-describedby="help-UID" value="60" min="0" max="6000" autocomplete="nofill"/><div class="form-group-icon"></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">Maximum MCP requests per minute from a single IP for unauthenticated (public persona) callers. Admin API key callers bypass this limit. Set to <code>0</code> to disable rate limiting for public callers.</p></div><div class="form-field text-field " data-type="text" style="--grid-area: toolPrefix;"><label for="field-UID">Tool Name Prefix</label><div class="form-group"><input type="text" id="field-UID" name="toolPrefix" placeholder="bistro" aria-describedby="help-UID" autocomplete="nofill"/><div class="form-group-icon"></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">Optional prefix prepended to every MCP tool name. Useful when running multiple T3 sites in the same AI agent so tools are distinguishable (e.g. <code>bistro</code> produces <code>bistro_list_collections</code>, <code>bistro_query_collection</code>). Leave blank for default names. Snake-case only, max 24 characters. <strong>Note:</strong> changing this requires connected AI clients (Claude Desktop, Inspector, etc.) to reconnect — existing sessions will see stale tool names until they re-initialize.</p></div><div class="form-field toggle-field " data-type="toggle" style="--grid-area: subscriptionsEnabled;"><label for="field-UID">Enable Resource Subscriptions</label><div class="form-group"><div class="switch"><input id="field-UID" name="subscriptionsEnabled" type="checkbox" aria-describedby="help-UID" checked/><label for="field-UID" aria-hidden="true">Enable Resource Subscriptions</label></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">When on, AI agents can call <code>resources/subscribe</code> on URIs like <code>tcms://blog/</code> and T3 will push <code>notifications/resources/updated</code> when matching content changes. Clients on the 2026-07-28 specification use <code>subscriptions/listen</code> instead, which T3 filters by the caller's access — an agent is only ever told about resources it could read directly. When off, the SDK still accepts subscribe calls but T3 won't push notifications, and <code>subscriptions/listen</code> is refused outright rather than holding a worker to deliver nothing — the kill switch for production troubleshooting.</p></div><div class="form-field number-field  field-hidden" data-type="number" style="--grid-area: subscriptionStreamSeconds; display: none;" data-settings="{&quot;visibility&quot;:{&quot;watch&quot;:&quot;subscriptionsEnabled&quot;,&quot;value&quot;:true,&quot;operator&quot;:&quot;==&quot;}}"><label for="field-UID">Subscription Stream Duration (seconds)</label><div class="form-group"><input type="number" id="field-UID" name="subscriptionStreamSeconds" inputmode="numeric" placeholder="1" aria-describedby="help-UID" value="1" min="1" max="30" autocomplete="nofill"/><div class="form-group-icon"></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">How long one <code>subscriptions/listen</code> stream stays open before closing on its own. That is the 2026-07-28 specification's replacement for <code>resources/subscribe</code>: a client opens one stream, names the resources it cares about, and receives updates until the window closes. <strong>Not the same as the Listening Stream Duration below</strong> — that one answers scanner probes with keepalives and nothing else, while this one carries real notifications, so there is an actual reason to hold it open. Each open stream holds one PHP worker for the whole window, and it shares the <em>Max Concurrent Listening Streams</em> ceiling below. Unlike the setting below, <strong>0 is not a cheap option here</strong> — it means <em>never close</em> to the underlying SDK, so anything at or below zero is ignored and 1 second is used instead. To stop serving these streams at all, turn off Enable Resource Subscriptions.</p></div><div class="form-field toggle-field " data-type="toggle" style="--grid-area: listeningStream;"><label for="field-UID">Answer SSE Probes with a Listening Stream</label><div class="form-group"><div class="switch"><input id="field-UID" name="listeningStream" type="checkbox" aria-describedby="help-UID" checked/><label for="field-UID" aria-hidden="true">Answer SSE Probes with a Listening Stream</label></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">Some MCP clients (e.g. OpenAI's plugin submission scanner) probe <code>/mcp</code> with a GET that asks for an SSE upgrade (<code>Accept: text/event-stream</code>) and treat the protocol-legal 405 response as a failure. When on, T3 answers those requests with a short keepalive-only SSE stream instead, auth- and Origin-checked identically to POST. A plain GET without the SSE Accept header always gets the normal 405, whatever this is set to. Each open stream holds one PHP-FPM worker for the duration below — turn this off under worker pressure and let the SDK return its normal 405.</p></div><div class="form-field number-field  field-hidden" data-type="number" style="--grid-area: listeningStreamSeconds; display: none;" data-settings="{&quot;visibility&quot;:{&quot;watch&quot;:&quot;listeningStream&quot;,&quot;value&quot;:true,&quot;operator&quot;:&quot;==&quot;}}"><label for="field-UID">Listening Stream Duration (seconds)</label><div class="form-group"><input type="number" id="field-UID" name="listeningStreamSeconds" inputmode="numeric" placeholder="1" aria-describedby="help-UID" value="1" min="0" max="30" autocomplete="nofill"/><div class="form-group-icon"></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">How long the listening stream (above) stays open before closing on its own. Clamped to 0-30 seconds no matter what's entered here. <strong>0 is the cheapest setting that still passes a probe</strong> — the client still gets a real 200 <code>text/event-stream</code> response with a keepalive event in it, but the worker is released in milliseconds instead of held for the whole window. Behind a buffering reverse proxy (Cloudflare, nginx with <code>proxy_buffering on</code>) the worker is held for the full duration even after the real client disconnects, so keep this as short as your scanning clients accept.</p></div><div class="form-field number-field " data-type="number" style="--grid-area: listeningStreamMaxConcurrent;"><label for="field-UID">Max Concurrent Listening Streams</label><div class="form-group"><input type="number" id="field-UID" name="listeningStreamMaxConcurrent" inputmode="numeric" placeholder="2" aria-describedby="help-UID" value="2" min="0" max="50" autocomplete="nofill"/><div class="form-group-icon"></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">Hard ceiling on streams opened site-wide per window, across all callers, covering both the listening stream above and <code>subscriptions/listen</code>. Both hold a PHP worker from the same pool, so they share one budget — separate caps would let the pair reach twice this number. This is the only limit that applies to OAuth-authenticated clients — the public rate limit above exempts anyone sending an <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> header — and the only one that bounds the total rather than a single IP. Keep it a small fraction of your <code>pm.max_children</code>: every worker a stream holds is taken from real request handling. Over the cap, probes get the normal 405 and subscription streams get a 503 telling them to retry. Set to <code>0</code> to disable the cap.</p></div><div class="form-field number-field  field-hidden" data-type="number" style="--grid-area: listeningStreamRetryMs; display: none;" data-settings="{&quot;visibility&quot;:{&quot;watch&quot;:&quot;listeningStream&quot;,&quot;value&quot;:true,&quot;operator&quot;:&quot;==&quot;}}"><label for="field-UID">Listening Stream Reconnect Delay (ms)</label><div class="form-group"><input type="number" id="field-UID" name="listeningStreamRetryMs" inputmode="numeric" placeholder="15000" aria-describedby="help-UID" value="15000" min="0" max="300000" autocomplete="nofill"/><div class="form-group-icon"></div></div><p class="help" id="help-UID">How long a client is told to wait before reconnecting after the stream closes, sent as the SSE <code>retry:</code> field. A client occupies a worker for the duration above, then idles for this long, so raising it lowers the steady-state cost of every connected client proportionally. Nothing is lost while disconnected: T3 never pushes unsolicited messages over this stream — subscription notifications are delivered on the client's next request. Some clients ignore this and reconnect immediately; those are bounded by the cap above.</p></div><div class="form-inline-fields"><button type="submit" class="button btn cms-save cms-button">Save Settings</button></div></form></div>